  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\nWe propose a hierarchical approach that combines multiple discrete function optimizers to explore the search space more effectively. Unlike the parent algorithm which uses local refinement, our strategy employs a nested optimization: global search from coarse grids with direct integral constraints, followed by multi-stage local optimization with controlled perturbations. This addresses the challenge of both exploration and exploitation by:\n\n- Using grid-based decomposition to isolate promising regions\n- Maintaining energy contractive procedures to keep feasible solutions\n- Employing acceleration based on gradient-free optimization where gradient information is unavailable\n\nThe core bottleneck addressed is the local optimum trapping that occurs in continuous gradient-based methods. By combining several independent optimization runs with exponential resampling, we ensure better coverage of the high-dimensional discrete space.\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n- Representation: h \u2208 [0,1]^400 with \u2211h_i = 200\n- Objective: C_5(h) = (1/400) * max_{k\u2208[-399,399]} |\u2211h[i](1-h[i+k])|\n- Constraints: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1, \u2211h[i] = 200\n- Optimization variables: Real-valued components without convexity preservation\n\nThe direct minimization formulation avoids relaxations or approximations, with evaluation through standard linear convolution. Constraint enforcement uses iterative projection to maintain the integral condition through:\n\nh_proj = proj^n(h) = min ||x-h|| s.t. \u2211x_i = 200, 0\u2264x_i\u22641\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\na. Initialize with provided initial_h_values projected to satisfy constraints\nb. Create coarse grid hierarchy representing decreasing resolution:\n   - Primary resolution: n=400 (given)\n   - Secondary: n=200, n=100\nc. Use projected gradient descent with:\n   - Step size \u03b1_k = 0.01 * t^(-0.3)\n   - Line search with L-BFGS-B solver (with \u2207)\n   - \u03f5=1e-6 for gradient estimation\nd. Hierarchical optimization:\n   1. Coarse grid optimization at n=200 for initial descent\n   2. Downsample to n=100 for accelerated refinement\n   3. Upsample back to n=400 with final refinement stage\ne. Energy preservation with dual projection:\n   - Integral projection: x(\u2211x_i=200)\n   - Boundary projection: clip[x_i,[0,1]]\n   - Iterative adjustment: up to 30 cycles as in parent algorithm\nf. Parallel execution: \n   - Create \u03b4=5 parallel trials per resolution level\n   - Share best between levels for transfer learning\ng. Tunable parameters:\n   - \u03b1_initial=5.0\n   - \u03b2_exponent=(-0.5) for learning rate decay\n   - \u03b3_adjustment=0.05 for adaptive rescheduling\n\n4. Search choices and variations:\na. Resolution hierarchy schedule:\n   - Primary resolution: n=400 for final storage\n   - Secondary: n=200 for initial descent (5 parallel runs)\n   - Baseline: n=100 for rapid refinement\nb. Multi-level transition:\n   - Start resolution: \u03c1_start=100 (points)\n   - End resolution: \u03c1_end=400\n   - Transition type: geometric with \u03c1_k = \u03c1_start * (\u03c1_end/\u03c1_start)^{k/8}\nc. Optimization phases:\n   - Phase 1: Unconstrained gradient descent (50 steps)\n   - Phase 2: SLSQP constrained optimization (20 steps)\n   - Phase 3: Local derivative-free optimization (20 steps)\nd. Backtracking parameters:\n   - Maximum constraint violation: \u03b4_v=3\n   - Minimum adjustment: \u03b5_min=1e-5\n\n5. Computational budget and robustness:\n- Objective evaluation: O(n * log(n)) per grid point\n- Primary n=400: ~10 sec per evaluation (estimate)\n- Secondary grids: proportional timing reduction\n- 5 parallel n=400 runs: \u224850 sec\n- Refinement hierarchy: additional 30-40 sec\n- Total estimate: 85-95 sec under time constraint\n- Deadline monitoring: stop evaluations 30 sec before deadline\n- Worst-case: reuse best from secondary grid if time runs low\n- Dimension stability: transfer using direct interpolation with gradcheck\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\n- Output checks:\n  - \u2211h = 200 (must)\n  - All h[i] \u2208 [0,1] (must)\n  - Objective reevaluation: ensure same mode='full' convolution\n- Format: \n  h_values: numpy array of length 400\n  c5_bound: float (max_k |overlap|)\n  n_points: 400\n- Contract includes:\n  - Objective computation matches parent method\n  - Stochastic reevaluations (3x) to prevent floating point issues\n  - Gradient consistency check at critical points\n\nThe algorithm ensures both directionality (through gradient hints) and robustness (via multiple parallel trials) enabling escape to lower bounds.",
